;; spec:   spec/types.md §6.5 (c), captures by closures (decided by the owner, 2026-10-24), the first `&` rule (case 18)
;; expect: accept
;; result: 0
;; audit:  clean
;; An `&` parameter may be captured by a non-escaping closure (case 08). Here the closure that captures it, `push`, is let-bound and
;; captured in its turn by the literal passed to `for-each`: before the decision that capture made `push` escaping, and the program
;; was rejected with `& parameter captured by escaping closure: v`; now `push` is a stack closure and the program runs. `dup-all`
;; appends every element twice through `push`; the write-back leaves the cell with nine elements whose sum is three times 6.
(defun dup-all (&v)
  (let ((push (fn (x) (append &v x))))
    (for-each @v (fn (x) (do (push x) (push x))))))

(defun main () -> i64
  (let ((v (cell [1 2 3])))
    (dup-all &v)
    (+ (if (= (count @v) 9) 0 1) (if (= (reduce + 0 @v) 18) 0 1))))
